Microservice Design Patterns:
-------------------------------
    when designing micrservices there are proven design solutions to common problems.

1. Decomposition Pattern:
-------------------------
   - we split into microservices based on Business Capability
       - Split based on domains
          - Authentication
          - Product Catalog
          - Payment Processing 
          - Order Processing
          (Domain driven design)

 2) Data Management Pattern
 -----------------------------
   Database per service.
      Lets say the below services has its own databases.
      EX:
            1. Login Page/Authentication. - Service - It authentication database
            2. Products catalog - service -  Products database 
            3. Payment Processing - service - Payments database 
            4. Order management. - service - Order management database

 3) Resillience Pattern:(Handling failures)
 -------------------------------------------
   Circuit breaker pattern: This prevents cascading failures.
   --------------------------
   Problem EX: EX: PC(product catalog) calling PP(payment processing) service, 
   lets say PC called PP and PP did not responded, now the PC thread calling PP will keep on hold.
   1000 members PC to PP, 1000 threads(process or 1000 webclient requests)  lets say if we dont get response for all these requests. the PC service will get crashed.
   Remedy : The circuit breaking pattern prevents this by failiing fast retrning an error immediately rather than waiting for request to respond.
   
   Retry pattern:
      PC a call went  to PP(capacity 500 requests) if PP capacity is full. Now PP returns error response saying i cant process your request. 
      PP processed some 10 request and now the PP (procesing 490 out of 500, now pp can hande 10 more requests) 
      PC service will send out the request one more time to PP(bcz there is capabilty to process 10 more requests) now my request will get succeded.

  Timout pattern:
     We send the request and wait for certain amount of time, if we get response in that time frame then it is fine, else the request is considered as failed.    


4) Depoyment Pattern:
----------------------
   Containerization: It is method f packaging an application and all its dependencies, libraries, binaries(java environemt, tomcat server ) 
   into a single isolated unit or file caled container
     Consistency: App Runs everywhere as in developers machine as well as in production also.
     EX: Docker.
   Orchestration: 
       If app grows, ex: from 1000 users to 1lakh users, i have to increase my servers. lets say to 100 machines each handling 1000 requests
     we use orchestrator to manage all these.
     It has automated scaling, managing the networking of all the machines.
      Kubernetes:
         automated scaling : Automatically removing and adding of servers based on traffic. 
         Self healing : If a conatiner fails, the orchestrator automatically restarts it, 
                    if the server not recovered yet after restarts then the system will be removed by orchestrator.
      Load balancing: Distributing incoming traffic eenly accross all containers.
   Canary Releases:
       Release to small % of users first.
       EX: release new code keep 10% user requests flow to new code and 90% request to old code.